#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107061 :tutorial/content "Let $O$ be a point on the plane. Let's consider all the points on the plane whose distance to $O$ is equal to $5\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\n![](images/ba/ba0fc787b4469b0322fa46fa072d58ae163e24dea284f3e5507306e9b30dfbf3.png)\n\nThe collection of all such points defines a geometrical shape which is called a **circle**.\n\n- The point $O$ is called the **center** of the circle.\n- The distance from $O$ to any point on the circle is called the **radius**.\n\nIn our diagram above, the radius is\n\n$$\nr={\\color{black}5\\,\\textrm{cm}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a53c8e3d-2c5a-558c-912f-f2e051ef7b41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2417 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107062 :tutorial/content "There are three important line segments associated with a circle:\n\n- A **radius** is any line segment that connects a point on the circle with the center (left-hand diagram).\n- A **chord** is any line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle (middle diagram).\n- A **diameter** is any chord that passes through the center of the circle (right-hand diagram). The diameter is the longest chord and its measure is twice the radius of the circle:\n$d = 2r$.\n\n![](images/7f/7f12a9db3210054e64397e26c87fd57ce4e55ff5dc9a2ed0d3b0ce3a8f9c2e65.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bcbdf407-5fe9-57d7-9359-167f8fbf21b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2418 :tutorial/title "Chords and Diameters"}]}
